%[text] # solve2PortNetwork
%[text] Solve the two-port network of the loudspeaker system for the diaphragm volume velocity and the source current
%[text] Class: lspsys
%%
%[text] ## Syntax
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] val = solve2PortNetwork(obj)
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] `val = solve2PortNetwork(obj)` multiplies the transmission matrices of the driver and of the acoustical side of the enclosure, `ta`, of the `lspsys` object `obj` for each frequency in `Frequency`, and solves the network for the diaphragm volume velocity and the source current. `createResult` calls this method; use `createResult` to get the full `result` object.
%%
%[text] ## Output Arguments
%[text] `val` — Solution of the network, returned as a structure with these fields:
%[text:table]
%[text] | Field | Symbol | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `DiaphragmVolumeVelocity` | $ U\_d $ | Volume velocity of the diaphragm in m3/s, row vector over `Frequency` |
%[text] | `SourceCurrent` | $ i\_g $ | Current of the source in A, row vector over `Frequency` |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Algorithms
%[text] The loudspeaker system is modelled as an equivalent circuit of the electrical, mechanical and acoustical domain. The symbols are listed in [Symbols](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','symbols.m'))).
%%
%[text] ### Transmission matrices
%[text] Every block of the circuit is a two-port network, described by its transmission matrix (ABCD matrix). The matrix relates the effort $ y $ (voltage, force or pressure) and the flow $ x $ (current, velocity or volume velocity) at port 1 to those at port 2. The effort is the quantity across the two terminals of a port; the flow enters the network at port 1 and leaves it at port 2, in the direction of the arrows:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:5b1e)
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{y\_1 \\cr x\_1} \\right\] = \\left\[ \\matrix{A & B \\cr C & D} \\right\] \\left\[ \\matrix{y\_2 \\cr x\_2} \\right\] $
%[text] Two-port networks in cascade multiply: the matrix of the chain is the product of the matrices of its blocks, from the source to the load.
%%
%[text] ### Equivalent circuit
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:4a7f)
%[text] The electrical and mechanical side are a chain of two-port networks: the electrical impedance $ T\_e $, the gyrator $ T\_{bl} $, the mechanical impedance $ T\_m $ and the transformer $ T\_{sd} $ from force and velocity to pressure and volume velocity. The acoustical side is the two-port network $ T\_a $, which holds 4-ports on three conductors: the front of the diaphragm (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear of the diaphragm (bottom). Port 2 of $ T\_a $ stays open: inside $ T\_a $, all flow goes to the ambient pressure.
%%
%[text] ### Electrical and mechanical side
%[text] Each block relates the quantities on its left to those on its right:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_g \\cr i\_g} \\right\] = T\_e \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_2 \\cr i\_2} \\right\], \\quad \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_2 \\cr i\_2} \\right\] = T\_{bl} \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_1 \\cr u\_1} \\right\], \\quad \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_1 \\cr u\_1} \\right\] = T\_m \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_2 \\cr u\_2} \\right\], \\quad \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_2 \\cr u\_2} \\right\] = T\_{sd} \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_1 \\cr U\_d} \\right\] $
%[text] so that
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_g \\cr i\_g} \\right\] = T\_e T\_{bl} T\_m T\_{sd} \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_1 \\cr U\_d} \\right\] $
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical side
%[text] $ T\_a $ is a two-port network like the other blocks. Port 1 is the front and the rear conductor: $ T\_{sd} $ floats between them, so its pressure is the pressure difference over the diaphragm, $ p\_1 = p\_{f1} - p\_{r1} $. The volume velocity $ U\_d $ leaves through the front conductor and returns through the rear conductor. Inside $ T\_a $, the 4-ports lead all flow to the ambient pressure: the reference symbols. Port 2 is the front and the rear conductor at the output, with the pressure difference $ p\_2 $ and the flow $ U\_2 $; it stays open, so $ U\_2 = 0 $. The flows are counted in the direction of the arrows: to the right on the front conductor and to the left on the rear conductor.
%[text] Inside $ T\_a $, the 4-port $ T\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ T\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation lie in cascade. Together they can model any linear enclosure and radiation, for example a closed box, a bass reflex or a horn, and radiation from the diaphragm, a port or a mouth:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:8c3d)
%[text] Each 4-port has a 4 × 4 transmission matrix. Its 2 × 2 blocks $ T\_{ff} $ and $ T\_{rr} $ describe what the 4-port does at the front and at the rear of the diaphragm, including elements to the ambient pressure; $ T\_{fr} $ and $ T\_{rf} $ describe coupling between front and rear, for example a leak or a vent, and are zero when the sides are independent:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{fk} \\cr U\_{fk} \\cr p\_{rk} \\cr U\_{rk}} \\right\] = \\left\[ \\matrix{T\_{ff} & T\_{fr} \\cr T\_{rf} & T\_{rr}} \\right\] \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{f,k+1} \\cr U\_{f,k+1} \\cr p\_{r,k+1} \\cr U\_{r,k+1}} \\right\] $
%[text] The two 4-ports multiply to one 4 × 4 matrix $ M = T\_{a,e} T\_{a,rad} $, with the rows $ m\_1 $ to $ m\_4 $. At the input, both flows are $ U\_d $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{f1} \\cr U\_d \\cr p\_{r1} \\cr U\_d} \\right\] = M x\_3, \\quad x\_3 = \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{f3} \\cr U\_{f3} \\cr p\_{r3} \\cr U\_{r3}} \\right\] $
%%
%[text] ### Transmission matrix of the acoustical side
%[text] At port 2, the pressure difference is $ p\_2 = p\_{f3} - p\_{r3} $ and both conductors carry the flow of the port, $ U\_{f3} = U\_{r3} = U\_2 $. The pressure $ p\_{r3} $ of the rear conductor against the reference is not fixed by the port. With $ w = \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr U\_2} \\right\] $ this is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ x\_3 = P w + q p\_{r3}, \\quad P = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1 \\cr 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1} \\right\], \\quad q = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 \\cr 0 \\cr 1 \\cr 0} \\right\] $
%[text] Rows 2 and 4 of $ M $ both give $ U\_d $, so their difference is zero. That fixes $ p\_{r3} $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p\_{r3} = -\\frac{(m\_2 - m\_4) P w}{(m\_2 - m\_4) q}, \\quad x\_3 = K w, \\quad K = P - \\frac{q (m\_2 - m\_4) P}{(m\_2 - m\_4) q} $
%[text] The difference of rows 1 and 3 gives $ p\_1 $ and row 2 gives $ U\_d $, which is the 2 × 2 transmission matrix of the acoustical side:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_1 \\cr U\_d} \\right\] = T\_a \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr U\_2} \\right\], \\quad T\_a = \\left\[ \\matrix{m\_1 - m\_3 \\cr m\_2} \\right\] K $
%%
%[text] ### Solution
%[text] All blocks are two-port networks, so the chain is one matrix product. Port 2 of $ T\_a $ is open, $ U\_2 = 0 $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_g \\cr i\_g} \\right\] = T \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr 0} \\right\], \\quad T = T\_e T\_{bl} T\_m T\_{sd} T\_a $
%[text] The first row of the chain gives $ p\_2 $ and the second row the source current; the diaphragm volume velocity follows from $ T\_a $. For each frequency:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p\_2 = \\frac{e\_g}{T\_{11}}, \\quad i\_g = T\_{21} p\_2, \\quad U\_d = T\_{a,21} p\_2 $
%[text] The pressures and flows inside $ T\_a $ follow from $ x\_3 = K w $ and the 4-ports, back to the input. The volume velocity that the 4-ports lead to the ambient pressure is the radiated volume velocity $ U\_r $, which `createResult` uses for the sound pressure.
%%
